{
  "id": "regions-cut-by-slashes",
  "questionId": "regions-cut-by-slashes",
  "slug": "regions-cut-by-slashes",
  "title": "Regions Cut By Diagonals",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "category": "Union find",
  "patterns": [
    "union find",
    "grid"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "split every cell into 4 triangles: top, right, bottom, left - a diagonal cut only separates some of these from each other, it doesn't touch neighboring cells directly",
    "for an empty cell (code 0), union all 4 of its triangles together since nothing separates them",
    "for a forward cut (code 1), union top with left, and separately union right with bottom - the cut itself keeps those two pairs apart",
    "for a backward cut (code 2), union top with right, and separately union left with bottom",
    "then handle the connections BETWEEN cells: union each cell's right triangle with its right neighbor's left triangle, and each cell's bottom triangle with its neighbor-below's top triangle",
    "the final answer is the number of distinct components across all 4 * n * n triangle nodes"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "trying to reason about the grid at the cell level instead of splitting each cell into 4 triangular nodes, which can't represent a cut correctly",
    "getting the top/right/bottom/left pairing backwards for '/' versus '\\', which silently produces a plausible-looking but wrong region count",
    "forgetting the cross-cell unions (right-to-left-neighbor, bottom-to-top-of-neighbor-below), which are what let separate cells' triangles merge into one region"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an n x n grid where each cell holds a code:\n\n- 0 means the cell is empty\n- 1 means the cell contains a forward diagonal cut (like a '/' running from its bottom-left corner to its top-right corner)\n- 2 means the cell contains a backward diagonal cut (like a '\\' running from its top-left corner to its bottom-right corner)\n\nTogether the cells form an n x n square, and the diagonal cuts divide that square into regions.\n\n**Return the total number of regions.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 30",
      "grid[i][j] is 0, 1, or 2"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a grid with no cuts at all (one single region)",
      "a single cell with one diagonal cut (splits into exactly 2 regions)",
      "cuts that line up across cell boundaries to form one long dividing line",
      "cuts that meet in the middle of the grid from four different cells"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "grid = [[0,1],[1,0]]",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "the two diagonal cuts line up to slice the square into 2 regions"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "grid = [[0,1],[0,0]]",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "a single diagonal cut in one corner cell is not enough to fully separate any region from the rest"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "grid = [[1,2],[2,1]]",
        "output": "5",
        "expectedOutput": "5",
        "explanation": "the four diagonal cuts meet in the middle like an X, carving out 5 separate regions"
      }
    ],
